course: dictionary
lesson: 16116
title: Whacking: hit, punish, or very big
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word whacking can mean three different things, so context tells you which one. Look at the words around it. «Mia kept whacking the pillow with her fist.» Here whacking means hitting something hard, with a loud smack.
explain: Whacking can also be an informal way to say very big. «The shop sent Dad a whacking bill for the new roof.» A bill cannot be hit, so whacking must mean very big. Try the swap: "a very big bill" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: The word also has an old-fashioned meaning: a beating given as a punishment. You will find this in very old books, and it is not used this way now. Today, hitting children is wrong and against the law in many countries, including Scotland, Wales and New Zealand.
fact: Whacking can mean hitting something hard with a loud smack.
fact: In British and Australian chat, «a whacking great...» means a really huge...
fact: Phrases such as «a whacking fine» mean a very big fine.
fact: The verb whack has the forms whacks, whacked and whacking.
fact: Hitting people, including children, is wrong, and it is against the law in many countries, including Scotland, Wales and New Zealand.
exercise: «Mia kept whacking the pillow with her fist.» What is Mia doing? | Tidying it up | Sleeping on it | Sewing a new cover | *Hitting it hard, with loud smacks
exercise: «The shop sent Dad a whacking bill for the new roof.» What does whacking mean here? | Very small | *Very big | Late | Hitting something
exercise: Ali says «a whacking fine» means a tiny fine. Is Ali right? | Yes, whacking means tiny | No, a fine is a kind of animal | *No, it means a very big fine | Yes, a fine is always tiny
test: «The whacking great wave soaked the whole beach.» What does whacking tell you about the wave? | It was very slow | *It was very big | It was very cold | It was very quiet
test: «The drummer was whacking the drum all night.» What does whacking mean here? | Resting on it | Carefully painting it | Singing softly | *Hitting it hard, with loud smacks
exam: Which sentence uses whacking to mean very big? | The dog was whacking its tail against the door. | *The bill was a whacking great one, far more than we expected. | Sam was whacking the ball over the fence. | The bat was whacking the table in anger.
